      <div class="panel providers" id="latest-current-comparison"><h3>Preserved dated comparison · B043 pre-study</h3><p class="subtle" id="latest-paired-note"></p><div class="data-scroll" id="latest-paired-table"></div><p class="gap-note" id="latest-paired-disagreements"></p></div>
      <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px" id="durable-report"><h3>Baseline reports survived the research failure.</h3><p class="coverage-copy" id="durable-report-copy"></p><p class="table-note">The checkpoint counts 46,525 game/model rows; the viewer adds four unresolved source rows, giving 46,529. These are model/version rows, not physical games. All 296 previously scored original games retain identical predictions and outcomes; growth to 329 scored games changes the aggregate population without improving frozen weights.</p></div>
      <details class="source-details"><summary>What failed, what completed, and what remains preserved</summary><p>B045 reached nine completed stages and failed during paired injury research after a bounded resource-admission wait. A later wrapper return recorded child exit −9 without the worker’s normal terminal return. The attached tail preserves work-progress evidence, but complete producer-fit counts, final resource totals and an external shell exit remain unknown in this snapshot. The signal alone does not establish an out-of-memory cause. At that earlier handoff, B046 was prepared for bounded recovery and explicit spread/ATS contracts; preparation received no owner-execution credit. The dated B046 execution section preserves that later result; the current B048 return and dated B047R1 status are reported above.</p><p>B044 had earlier stopped at the first source-refresh handoff because its acquisition consumer did not accept the authenticated predecessor release. It completed zero of sixteen stages and committed no new model fits or forecasts. B045 was then prepared with an exact producer-consumer preflight and acquisition handoff repair; that preparation and the earlier missing-clock repair remain dated evidence.</p><p>B043 completed nine of sixteen stages and preserved baseline reports before injury research. An unknown prediction-row label clock reached strict checkpoint JSON as NaT, stopping the injury stage before completed ablations. B044 normalizes missing membership clocks to null without inventing dates or weakening training-label release rules. Historical archive, in-game acquisition and market-path research were not reached; no scientific gain is inferred from repair tests.</p><p>B042 completed nine stages before its injury base verifier encountered a last-bit difference between two floating-point reductions. The B043 repair matches the original producer exactly and retains the equality guard; it does not rewrite predictions or loosen cutoffs. The injury study produced no new challenger fits. Five later stages, including final issue reconciliation and scorecards, were not started.</p><p>The B041 owner remains a separate setup failure: independent predecessor queues reused one progress counter, and the monotonic guard stopped setup before science. B040 earlier completed six of thirteen stages, retained 557,737 observations and reused 108 historical targets. These dated outcomes stay in the evidence history. Recovery and a prepared repair do not turn any failed owner into a completed run.</p><p>The earlier B033 attempt restored its parent, built historical features and completed all 108 registered target fits across the two leagues. A shared-summary selector then encountered a summary belonging to another project and stopped the current-source refresh. The incomplete refresh produced no fresh current-game or provider coverage. The committed historical artifacts and the verified return remain preserved.</p><p>B035 later stopped before science with 510 passed fixtures, three errors and one skip. B036 corrected that portability problem and completed the workflow. It still recorded 417 current boxscore normalization errors, exchange identity gaps, a Kalshi rate-limit halt, and no configured runtime historical-odds credential. The earlier B032 attempt had failed at the streaming-reader boundary with zero completed scientific stages. That failure remains in the evidence history. Its later durability verification did not add scientific credit. The B033 stream repair allowed real work to proceed; neither repair nor test success implies full coverage or a market advantage.</p></details>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="research-status" aria-labelledby="research-title"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">Research in preparation</div><h2 id="research-title">Coverage first. Claims after measurement.</h2></div><p>B046 committed substantial injury candidate work, then deferred at its shared-memory gate. Completed paired studies and demonstrated injury or path gains remain separate requirements. Dated B043 evidence is preserved.</p></div><div class="track-grid"><article class="track"><span class="badge">INJURY STUDY · INCOMPLETE</span><h3>Injury evidence and forecast time</h3><p id="injury-copy"></p><p class="table-note" id="injury-clocks"></p><p class="table-note" id="injury-policy"></p></article><article class="track"><span class="badge">MARKET PATHS · NOT REACHED</span><h3>Chronological market-path research</h3><p id="frontier-copy"></p><p class="table-note" id="frontier-limit"></p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section" id="spread-review" aria-labelledby="spread-title"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">Spread probabilities and ATS records</div><h2 id="spread-title">Winning the game is a different question.</h2></div><p>A cover probability describes one quoted line. An against-the-spread record describes prior settled comparisons. This example shows both with invented inputs.</p></div>
      <div class="engineering"><article class="panel"><h3>Change the line. Keep the model fixed.</h3><p class="subtle">Invented full-game home-minus-away margin distribution, including overtime. The home win probability stays 75%.</p><div class="demo-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Invented spread reference"><button type="button" data-spread="favorite_half" aria-pressed="true">Home −3.5</button><button type="button" data-spread="favorite_integer" aria-pressed="false">Home −3</button><button type="button" data-spread="underdog_half" aria-pressed="false">Home +3.5</button></div><div class="coverage-grid" id="spread-probabilities" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="table-note" id="spread-orientation"></p><p class="table-note">Cover when home margin + home spread is positive; push at zero; fail to cover when negative. Push probability is separate, so the away cover probability need not equal one minus home cover probability.</p><p class="gap-note">These are invented probability masses, not a fitted NCAA model or a live price. A real probability needs a validated margin distribution, exact teams, gender, line, horizon, source version, information clocks and settlement scope. A point margin or winner probability alone cannot supply it.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Read an ATS record with its denominator.</h3><div class="coverage-grid" id="ats-record"></div><p class="table-note" id="ats-denominator"></p><p class="table-note">The invented inputs include one result with an unknown availability clock. It remains excluded; the missing clock is never replaced with capture time. Only earlier completed games known before the target cutoff can enter a team’s prior record.</p><p class="gap-note">The record is descriptive. Its win rate is not the next game’s cover probability. Home and away orientation, provider, US/international product, horizon, evidence tier, pushes and gaps remain separate. Each exact quoted line and its availability must be retained.</p><div class="demo-output" id="spread-clock-proof" role="status"></div><a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/blob/main/examples/ncaa_review/spread_ats.py">Run the exact spread and ATS example ↗</a></article></div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="engineering" aria-labelledby="engineering-title"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">03 / Inspect the engineering</div><h2 id="engineering-title">Move a clock. Watch the contract.</h2></div><p>This interaction uses invented inputs only. The executable Python example tests the same classes of failure without credentials, subscriptions or cloud access.</p></div><div class="engineering"><div class="panel"><h3>Information available before 16:00 UTC</h3><p class="subtle">Invented tipoff: 18:00 UTC · decision horizon: two hours.</p><div class="demo-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Invented contract scenarios"><button type="button" data-scenario="valid" aria-pressed="true">All inputs in time</button><button type="button" data-scenario="late_quote" aria-pressed="false">Late quote</button><button type="button" data-scenario="boundary_label" aria-pressed="false">Boundary label</button><button type="button" data-scenario="settlement" aria-pressed="false">Wrong settlement</button></div><div class="demo-clocks"><div><span>Latest training label</span><strong id="demo-label">15:00 UTC</strong></div><div><span>Quote available</span><strong id="demo-quote">15:55 UTC</strong></div><div><span>Feature available</span><strong>15:10 UTC</strong></div><div><span>Settlement scope</span><strong id="demo-settlement">Full game + overtime</strong></div></div><div class="demo-output" id="demo-output" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="table-note">Passing an identity comparison does not establish forecast quality. The public example separately checks source and product semantics.</p></div><div class="panel"><h3>Reproduce the review mechanism.</h3><p class="subtle">Python 3.11+ · standard library · deterministic invented data.</p><pre class="code">python examples/ncaa_review/contracts.py --demo
python examples/ncaa_review/progress.py
python examples/ncaa_review/handoff.py --demo
python examples/ncaa_review/spread_ats.py
python -m unittest discover -s examples/ncaa_review -p 'test_*.py'
python examples/ncaa_review/build_dashboard.py --check</pre><ul class="checklist"><li>Reject future and exact-boundary training labels.</li><li>Pair each provider on the exact same games and products.</li><li>Keep frozen, reconstructed and issued tracks separate.</li><li>Preserve both winning and losing disagreements.</li><li>Verify complete streamed bytes and close the actual reader.</li></ul><a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/tree/main/examples/ncaa_review">Read the source, tests and methodology ↗</a></div></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="progress" aria-labelledby="progress-title"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">04 / Invented progress contract</div><h2 id="progress-title">An estimate needs evidence.</h2></div><p>Switch the invented scenario to inspect counts and a measured phase ETA. This is a reproducible demonstration, not a connection to a running owner workload.</p></div><div class="panel"><div class="demo-buttons progress-buttons" id="progress-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Invented progress scenarios"></div><div class="progress-demo-grid"><div><span class="progress-key">Durably completed units</span><div class="progress-count" id="progress-count"></div><div class="progress-track" id="progress-track" role="progressbar" aria-label="Invented task completion"><span id="progress-fill"></span></div><div class="progress-subline" id="progress-components"></div></div><div id="progress-output" role="status" aria-live="polite"><span class="progress-key">Estimated time remaining in this phase</span><div class="progress-eta" id="progress-eta"></div><div class="progress-range" id="progress-range"></div><div class="progress-subline" id="progress-clocks" style="margin-top:12px"></div></div></div><p class="progress-note" id="progress-explanation"></p><p class="table-note">Restored units reduce remaining work without inflating new-work throughput. Unknown totals, warmup, a changed worker count or a stalled pace produce an explicit unavailable estimate. A measured pace range is not a confidence interval or a promise about later phases.</p><a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/blob/main/examples/ncaa_review/progress.py">Inspect the executable progress contract ↗</a></div></section>
    <section class="section" id="preflight" aria-labelledby="preflight-title"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">05 / Invented portable validation</div><h2 id="preflight-title">Finished checks are not all passes.</h2></div><p>Unsupported optional integration, required capability and unexpected errors have different outcomes. No process, provider or model is accessed by this example.</p></div><div class="panel"><div class="demo-buttons" id="preflight-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Invented preflight scenarios"></div><div class="demo-output" id="preflight-output" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><div class="coverage-grid" id="preflight-counts"></div><p class="table-note" id="preflight-explanation"></p><a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/blob/main/examples/ncaa_review/preflight.py">Inspect the portable validation contract ↗</a></div></section>
    <section class="section" id="handoff" aria-labelledby="handoff-title"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">06 / Invented provenance contract</div><h2 id="handoff-title">Test the handoff itself.</h2></div><p>A producer and a consumer can each pass their own tests while their actual handoff remains incompatible. Validate their exact shared contract before work begins.</p></div><div class="panel"><div class="data-scroll"><table><caption>Invented results generated by the executable example</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Observed result</th></tr></thead><tbody id="handoff-table"></tbody></table></div><p class="table-note">The reviewed registration binds project, release, run, source digest, canonical artifact prefix, wire format and exact content pins. Mixed identities, conflicting registrations and rehashed payload substitutions are rejected. This example assumes an authenticated registry and supplies no private owner or model evidence.</p><a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/blob/main/examples/ncaa_review/handoff.py">Inspect the handoff contract and adversarial tests ↗</a></div></section>
    <details class="source-details"><summary>Aggregate provenance and public reproducibility boundary</summary><p>The historical objective and season metrics were independently recomputed from authenticated per-game exports. The public report contains aggregates, calibration bins and descriptive uncertainty. It excludes private data, individual forecasts, feature transforms, active model configurations, weights and execution handoffs. The generic invented example is reproducible here; private real-data retraining is not.</p><p>Accepted historical owner-return SHA-256:<br><span class="hash" id="history-hash"></span></p><p>Preserved B043 research owner-return SHA-256:<br><span class="hash" id="failed-hash"></span></p><p><a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/blob/main/tracks/__LEAGUE__/evidence.json">Inspect the exact public aggregate JSON ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/blob/main/docs/PUBLICATION_BOUNDARY.md">Publication boundary ↗</a></p></details>
    <p class="print-note">Snapshot: __REVIEW_DATE__. Historical research is development-exposed; no prospective market advantage is claimed.</p>
